whose boys have been to camp several times,

263
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
they spend a very short amount of time in the campsite.

264
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
They usually get down there.

265
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
They kind of unload the Scout,

266
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
They say goodbye and they head back up and head home.

267
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Parents of Scouts who are there for their first year

268
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,000
perhaps will spend a little bit more time,

269
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:36,000
because this may be the first time

270
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:39,000
that their son is going to be away from them

271
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
for that long a period of time.

272
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
Their son is going to be out of their orbit,

273
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
And you know what It's. a little worrisome, isn't it?

274
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:51,000
And I've watched this with parents for 30 years now,

275
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
And so if you're one of those parents,

276
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
I want to speak to you a little bit

277
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,000
about what I see from my side

278
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
and what happens during the week of summer camp.

279
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
I will tell you, without a doubt,

280
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:06,000
that your son is going to come home from summer camp

281
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
more capable and mature than he was

282
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
when you waved goodbye to him,

283
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
Outside of the normal support structure

284
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
of his parents and his family

285
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,000
and away from all of those solitary distractions

286
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
that bother us in one way or another.

287
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
He's away from the internet.

288
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
He's away from his computer games,

289
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
He's away from the television set.

290
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
He's got to take care of himself

291
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
and cooperate with his peers.

292
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
He's got to interact with people.

293
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:35,000
He does all this in a very supportive environment

294
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
that permits him to try things out

295
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
and maybe get them wrong,

296
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
without compromising his safety.

297
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
But he's going to be challenged

298
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
to expand his abilities

299
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
to accept and offer help

300
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
and to function cooperatively in everyday tasks.

301
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
If you think about it,

302
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
what your scout does at camp

303
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
is pretty amazing,

304
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
And he does 99% of this

305
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
without the mediation

306
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
of an adult reminding him to do so.

307
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
He is challenged to be responsible

308
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
not only for himself

309
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
but for his fellow scouts.

310
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
So he's going to get up in the morning on schedule.

311
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
He's going to be sharing a tent

312
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
with another scout

313
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
and they're going to need to keep that tent

314
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
reasonably neat and clean.

315
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
He's going to share chores with his fellow scouts

316
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
to maintain the campsite,

317
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
Depending on where he goes to camp.

318
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
at some scout camps

319
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
they serve meals in the campsite.

320
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
At some there's a dining hall

321
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
or has a dining hall,

322
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
And he's going to share three meals a day,

323
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
served basically family style

324
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
meals.

325
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
It's been some years since we had a scout in our house,

326
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
but I know that

327
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
when he was in middle school and high school

328
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
at least, we felt pretty fortunate

329
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
to have two or three meals a week

330
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
where we were all sitting at the same table.

331
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
But this is going to happen: three meals a day

332
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
for every day that he's at camp-

333
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
And you know what Grace is going to be said.

334
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
he's going to have his opportunity

335
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
to serve as a waiter for his table

336
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
and serving and cleaning up after meals.

337
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
He's going to manage and budget

338
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
given him for camp.

339
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
He's going to set and follow a schedule

340
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
of activities according to his interests.

341
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
He's going to be responsible

342
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
to find his way to and from those activities

343
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
and manage his time.

344
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
He's going to build character

345
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and values by attending campfires

346
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
and ceremonies

347
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and reflecting on those activities.

348
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
He's going to find himself complimented

349
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
many times by the older scouts

350
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
that are charged with his well-being.

351
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
His opinions are actually going to be

352
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
heard, and heard by other scouts and adults.

353
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
He's going to have the opportunity

354
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
to take on real responsibility

355
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
at times

356
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
and to be the leader of other scouts.

357
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
He's going to make new friends.

358
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
He's going to strengthen his existing friendships.

359
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
He's going to learn new skills.

360
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
He's going to try something

361
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
he's probably never tried before.

362
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
He's going to become more aware

363
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
of the natural environment that we live in.

364
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
He's going to challenge

365
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
his critical thinking skills.

366
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
He's going to put what he learns

367
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
into action. He's going to work with others

368
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
to establish and achieve goals.

369
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
He's going to be challenged to

370
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
apply himself to contribute

371
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
to achieving those goals.

372
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
He's going to trade all of those

373
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
solitary pursuits like TV and video games

374
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
and all of that

375
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
for actual social interaction.

376
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
He's going to have to find

377
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
a healthy resolution to conflicts

378
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
and he's going to benefit from

379
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
a tremendous positive peer pressure.

380
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
following the good example

381
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
of older scouts and counselors.

382
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Now, summer camp

383
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
for a young scout

384
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
is going to be challenging,

385
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
but it's also an adventure

386
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
that's full of possibilities.

387
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
It's an opportunity for parents

388
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
to let him go a little,

389
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
Let him stand on his own two feet,

390
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
and when you do that,

391
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
that's an expression of confidence and trust

392
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
in his abilities

393
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
and it's really a test of your parenting.

394
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and parents,

395
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
I find, are usually

396
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
a little less prepared

397
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
for summer camp than their sons

398
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
are. We will spend a lot of time

399
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
working with them to make sure that

400
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
they're ready. There is gear and clothing

401
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
and things like that to gather.

402
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
We may not appreciate

403
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
what it's going to be like at home

404
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
when our boy is off to summer camp.

405
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
It may be an uncomfortable

406
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
feeling turning over full

407
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
time care to

408
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
others that you maybe just have gotten

409
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
to know. Perhaps you're going to worry

410
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
about the kind of food that they're going to serve,

411
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
who's going to be looking after

412
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
your daily concerns, like clean clothes

413
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
and taking a shower,

414
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
and how the older scouts are going to treat him-

415
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
and that's a worry for every parent.

416
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
is he going to be teased?

417
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
are they going to be tough and mean to him?

418
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
and we're going to be worried if.

419
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
what? if he doesn't like

420
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
being at camp. Is he going to be able to call

421
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
home? or can we call

422
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
him and check in with him?

423
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
and all of these questions cause us a little bit of anxiety,

424
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
and you know what

425
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
a lot of times

426
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
we parents have a harder time

427
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
with the experience of sending

428
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
our sons off to scout camp

429
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
than they ever will- at camp,

430
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
I mean.

431
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and that first letter home, if we even get

432
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
one, can sound kind of miserable

433
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and we try to read between the lines

434
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
and, yeah, it can be

435
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
a distressing time for us

436
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
as parents. so I want to help

437
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
you prepare yourself for

438
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
this experience. if this is the

439
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
first time you're going to be separated from

440
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
your child for this

441
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
length of time, it's a new challenge

442
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
and, even though many others have gone

443
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
through it before, it's something

444
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
that's new to you, and the first

445
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
step is really acknowledging

446
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
that this is possibly

447
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
possibly going to cause you some

448
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
distress, no matter how much

449
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
I talk about it, no matter

450
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
how many other parents have been through it.

451
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
this challenge of being separated

452
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
from your child for this length of time

453
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
could be new to you,

454
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
but you know what. a lot of us

455
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
have gone through it before and

456
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
we know that it gets better. so don't worry

457
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
if you feel distressed or anxious,

458
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
it's pretty normal. it's a pretty

459
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
normal reaction, but it's temporary

460
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
and you're going to get over it

461
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and you're going to feel better. now I will tell

462
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
you that most younger scouts

463
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
are going to encounter

464
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
some amount of homesickness.

465
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
they're going to have some ups

466
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
and downs because if camp

467
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
is going to reap the benefits that

468
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
we want from it, it's going to be challenging

469
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
for them. they're not going to be

470
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
abjectly miserable,

471
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
but they're going to find things

472
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
challenging and all of this

473
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
is really a part

474
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
of a healthy process of growing up

475
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
that scouts are going to encounter

476
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
one way or another

477
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
at one time or another.

478
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
if they're 11 or 12 years old

479
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
and they're off for a week at summer camp

480
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
without you, they're going to

481
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
encounter some of the same challenges

482
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
that are going to happen

483
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
when, after they've graduated high school,

484
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
which I know seems an impossibly

485
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
distant thing in the future,

486
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
but I can tell you it's right around the corner.

487
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
after they've graduated high school

488
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
and they've gone off to college,

489
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
they're going to be encountering the same things that they

490
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
encountered at camp, maybe on a bigger scale

491
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
and for a longer period of separation

492
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
from home. so getting

493
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
over that now, dealing

494
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
with it now, is a very

495
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
good thing for them to be able to do.

496
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
a couple of ways we can prepare ourselves

497
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
for this time that our

498
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
children are away at camp

499
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
is to think positively.

500
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
I know that sounds very trite,

501
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
doesn't it? but scouts

502
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
typically thrive

503
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
at summer camp and they come home happy.

504
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
and when you're talking to

505
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
your scout about camp,

506
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
instead of discussing your worries

507
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
and how much you're going to miss them,

508
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
talk about all

509
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
of the great things that are going to happen at camp

510
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
and the great new experiences they're going to have.

511
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
keep everything on a positive level-

512
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
and of course

513
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
you're going to miss them.

514
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
of course it's going to be difficult for you,

515
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
but one of the ways that you

516
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
can kind of help your scout

517
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
avoid feeling really homesick

518
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
for camp when you're discussing it with your scout,

519
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
stay positive

520
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
for a long time scout master.

521
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
I am always ready

522
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
to answer questions about summer camp

523
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
and there aren't any silly questions.

524
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
really, it's always better

525
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
to ask than to worry,

526
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
and a lot of parents worry

527
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
about things that

528
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
they may consider silly.

529
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
well, what if he doesn't like the food?

530
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
what if he runs out of clean clothes?

531
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
what if he runs out of money?

532
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
what if he really doesn't like it? there

533
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
is there a way that he can call us,

534
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
but we have to come and pick him up.

535
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
you know, ask those questions,

536
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
and there may be a lot of questions

537
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
that are just kind of specific

538
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
to a given summer camp

539
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
or given scout troop that I won't

540
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
have the answers to here.

541
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
so ask the people who are going to camp

542
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
with them. there really aren't any silly questions

543
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
and it's always better

544
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
to ask than to worry about the answers.

545
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
right now I will tell you.

546
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
one of the things you're going to want to understand

547
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
is how you'll be

548
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
able to communicate with your scout

549
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
while he's at camp,

550
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
and most of the time this is going to be

551
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
pretty minimal communication.

552
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
before the advent of cell phones

553
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
and things like that, it was almost exclusively

554
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
one way communication,

555
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
when the scout

556
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
was encouraged or compelled

557
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
to send a letter or a postcard

558
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
home. usually you can mail

559
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
a letter or a card for them at camp.

560
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
I know at our camp you can

561
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
email messages to scouts

562
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
that could be helpful for you

563
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
talking to them on the phone.

564
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
well, you know what it all depends.

565
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
I don't have a

566
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
blanket policy and I don't have

567
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
blanket advice for how to

568
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
manage that yourself.

569
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
I will say that generally

570
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
it's a better idea

571
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
to minimize those types of communications.

572
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
I will address home sickness

573
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
and scouts by making sure

574
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
that they can contact home

575
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
and talk to home,

576
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
and in my experience that actually

577
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
helps. over time

578
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
we got to remember that every experience

579
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
and every set of challenges is an individual one.

580
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
so the way that these communications

581
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
are going to work

582
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
and exactly what you'll be able to do

583
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
and what you won't be able to do

584
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
is something that you should ask questions about,

585
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
and you should also prepare your scout for that.

586
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
unless your

587
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
summer camp is very close by,

588
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
like ours is,

589
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
it may not be very likely that you're going to get a chance

590
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
to visit camp. if you do get a chance

591
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
to visit, there are going to be some rules

592
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
associated with that visit

593
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
and it's something that you want to familiarize

594
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
yourself with at our particular

595
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
camp on a Saturday, when parents

596
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
can visit, we can have several hundred

597
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
extra people in camp and you can imagine

598
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
that that can be a little stressful

599
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
for the camp staff and even for the adults

600
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
that are down with a scout troop.

601
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
so think very carefully

602
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
about the impact your visits going to have

603
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
and be respectful of the rules

604
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
and the common practices around that.

605
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
and finally, to get ready

606
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
to send your son off to camp,

607
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
think about planning something special for yourself.

608
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
you're having a little mini vacation

609
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
from being a parent

610
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
for a few days.

611
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
plan something interesting or special

612
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
that you couldn't otherwise do,

613
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
and make that happen while they're away at camp.

614
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
as with most other things in life,

615
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
this whole experience of being a parent

616
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,000
and sending your scout off to camp

617
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
really has a lot to do with

618
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
the way that you've prepared yourself,

619
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
the way that you've prepared your scout

620
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
and the attitude with which

621
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
you approach the entire thing.

622
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
there's going to be challenging times, for certain.

623
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
there's going to be challenging times for your

624
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
scout at camp.

625
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
there's going to be challenging times for you at home.

626
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
but I would have to say that,

627
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
based on my experience over 30 years

628
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
of doing this,

629
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
when you meet these challenges with a positive attitude,

630
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
both

631
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
yourself at home as a parent

632
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
going through this experience

633
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
and your scout at camp going through this experience.

634
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
you're both going to benefit from it.

635
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
the ninth point of the scout law:

636
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
quick, quick,

637
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
thrifty.

638
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
I have a fondness for words-

639
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
I really do- especially uncommon or obscure words

640
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
that have been around for a long time.

641
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
I find that words like this

642
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
creep into my conversation

643
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
very often

644
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
and when I talk like this

645
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
around my scouts

646
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
I usually get a confused look from the younger scouts

647
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
and the older scouts kind of glance at each other

648
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
as if to say there he goes again,

649
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
another one of those $9 college words

650
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
that nobody knows what it means.

651
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
but anyway,

652
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
thrifty is one of those

653
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
great old words,

654
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
a word you don't hear every day,

655
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
and I get a kick out of talking to scouts

656
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
in a scout masters conference

657
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
and asking them what they think thrifty means.

658
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
it's kind of like art link letters.

659
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
the things kids say or whatever that show was.

660
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
their answers are all

661
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
over the map and sometimes they're a little entertaining.

662
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
but understand

663
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
the fact that we use the word

664
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
thrifty in the scout law.

665
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
it would be a great opportunity to be able to discuss

666
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
the concepts behind it

667
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
with scouts,

668
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
because it's not a very common word.

669
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
now, I'm not suggesting that we ever dump

670
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
good old thrifty for something new

671
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
or something updated.

672
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
I think we need to keep thrifty around,

673
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
like we need to keep old men like me around,

674
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
because we're quirky and entertaining.

675
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
if you want to help scouts understand

676
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
thrifty,

677
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
then ask them what it means to be resourceful.

678
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
resourceful is not exactly a synonym,

679
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
but it's pretty close, isn't it?

680
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
in the handbook, the ninth point of the scout law

681
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
is explained this way:

682
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
a scout works to pay his own way

683
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
and to help others,

684
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
he saves for the future,

685
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
he protects and conserves natural resources

686
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
and he uses his time

687
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
and property wisely.

688
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
now we know the scout law isn't just for

689
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
scouts, it's there for us too.

690
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
so how, in our role as

691
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
scouts, are we thrifty

692
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
or resourceful?

693
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
I do that question in many ways, to be sure,

694
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
but I'm thinking about a certain

695
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
aspect of resourcefulness,

696
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
a daily opportunity for our scouts

697
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
to gain a useful life skill.

698
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
and to illustrate that,

699
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
I'm reminded of a cold, wet,

700
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
long November night

701
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
guiding a youth group over Indian Pass

702
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
in New York's Adirondack

703
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
Park right there in the high peaks region.

704
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
it was not my favorite trip ever,

705
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
but it was pretty memorable

706
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
as we headed over Indian Pass.

707
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:42,000
it was November, it was cold,

708
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
it started to sleet and to snow

709
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
and all but one of our

710
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
flashlights gave out.

711
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
so the only way to make progress

712
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
on this rocky, steep trail

713
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
was to light the trail ahead.

714
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
I had the only working light

715
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
and so

716
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
I would go up ahead of the group

717
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
and I would shine that light on the trail

718
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
and they would hike the twenty yards

719
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
or so up until they caught up with me,

720
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
and then I would go forward again

721
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
and they would catch up.

722
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
and so we kind of leapfrogged our way

723
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
over several miles of really tough terrain

724
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
in a slow,

725
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
steady slog well into

726
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
the early morning hours

727
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
before we got to our campsite.

728
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
our role in scouting

729
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
is similar: we illuminate the trail ahead

730
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
and we wait for the scouts to kind of catch up

731
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
with us and then we illuminate some more of the trail.

732
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
and the way that we do this

733
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
is by being resourceful

734
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
and thrifty with our words,

735
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
by asking good

736
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
questions,

737
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
because we want scouts

738
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
to learn to be resourceful

739
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
and thrifty themselves,

740
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
to find things out for themselves.

741
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
so asking a

742
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
well formed question opens

743
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
the doors of opportunity to them.

744
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
we tap the resourcefulness

745
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
of our scouts and appeal

746
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
to their sense of curiosity,

747
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
we get them up and active,

748
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
we ignite a flame of inquiry

749
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
when we are thrifty

750
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
with our words

751
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
and we're resourceful by asking them questions.

752
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
and where are the answers to those questions?

753
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
a lot of them end up being

754
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
within ourselves,

755
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,000
some are beyond ourselves,

756
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
but a lot of them cause us to look deep

757
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
into ourselves

758
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
and when we do that

759
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
we learn more about ourselves.

760
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
and when we do that in a positive environment,

761
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
we develop the character that is

762
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
the aim of scouting.

763
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
now, it is thrifty, but so are scouts.

764
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
be thrifty,

765
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
be resourceful

766
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
and light the way forward for your scouts

767
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
by asking good questions.

